From: Adriel Santos <adriel@adriel.eu>
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5_mpd: tear down the user PDs even when respawn is suppressed

0823 does the SCM half of a user PD stop (internal radio shutdown for
asid 1, MSA unlock) for every up user PD when the root PD crashes, but
only when a respawn is allowed. With respawn_userpds=0, or when the rate
limit suppresses the respawn (a crash within 60 s of the last recovery,
or respawn_max_per_hour reached), it returned before the teardown: the
root then went through pas_shutdown and a fresh load with the internal
radio still powered and both user PD MSA regions still locked, an order
the vendor stack never uses. The owner's later stop then did that SCM
half against the new root firmware instance, through the stale-generation
path.

On the bench that path completed for both user PDs after a suppressed
respawn. The board reset seen when pd-1 was restarted that way was an
ath11k oops in the restart work (its failed crash recovery had freed the
HAL, see ath11k patch 961), not these SCM calls. Still, the root's
restart should not depend on user PD state that the firmware instance
which granted it no longer knows about.

Always tear the up user PDs down with the crashed root and let the rate
limit gate only the respawn. A PD that is not respawned stays torn_down,
so its owner's later stop takes the existing torn_down path (no stop
handshake, no second unlock) and its start is a cold boot.

The stale-generation check in wcss_pd_stop() is then only a guard, so
update the comments that still send suppressed PDs down that path.

--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_mpd.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_mpd.c
@@ -47,10 +47,9 @@
 
 /*
  * A root PD crash takes every user PD down with it, but the remoteproc core
- * only recovers the root. When set, the user PDs that were up are torn down
- * together with the root and spawned again once it is back. When clear, they
- * are left for their owner (ath11k, sysfs) to stop and start; the stop then
- * skips the handshake the restarted firmware cannot answer.
+ * only recovers the root. The user PDs that were up are torn down together
+ * with the root. When set, they are spawned again once it is back. When clear,
+ * they stay down until their owner (ath11k, sysfs) stops and starts them.
  */
 static bool respawn_userpds = true;
 module_param(respawn_userpds, bool, 0644);
@@ -63,8 +62,8 @@
  * crash, every few seconds, and the remoteproc core has no limit of its own.
  * So the respawn is suppressed when the root crashes within RESPAWN_HOLDOFF
  * of its last crash recovery, and once respawn_max_per_hour respawns have
- * been done within RESPAWN_PERIOD. The user PDs are then left to the stale
- * path, as with respawn_userpds=0.
+ * been done within RESPAWN_PERIOD. The user PDs then stay down, as with
+ * respawn_userpds=0.
  */
 #define RESPAWN_HOLDOFF		(60 * HZ)
 #define RESPAWN_PERIOD		(3600 * HZ)
@@ -393,8 +392,12 @@
  * This runs before the root's own pas_shutdown, the same order as a normal
  * stop of every user PD followed by the root.
  *
- * With respawn disabled or suppressed nothing is torn down, and the user PDs
- * are left for the stale path. Returns whether a respawn is owed.
+ * The teardown happens whether or not a respawn is allowed: with respawn
+ * disabled or suppressed the PDs simply stay torn down, and their owner's
+ * later stop/start takes the torn_down path. Leaving them up instead let the
+ * root restart with the internal radio still powered and the MSA regions
+ * still locked, an order the vendor stack never uses.
+ * Returns whether a respawn is owed.
  */
 static bool q6_wcss_teardown_userpds(struct q6_wcss *wcss)
 {
@@ -413,10 +416,11 @@
 		owed = true;
 	}
 
-	if (!owed || !q6_wcss_respawn_allowed(wcss)) {
+	if (!owed) {
 		mutex_unlock(&wcss->upd_lock);
 		return false;
 	}
+	owed = q6_wcss_respawn_allowed(wcss);
 
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(wcss->upd); i++) {
 		upd = wcss->upd[i];
@@ -444,7 +448,7 @@
 	}
 	mutex_unlock(&wcss->upd_lock);
 
-	return true;
+	return owed;
 }
 
 /* The cold boot order: wcss_pd_load(), then the body of wcss_pd_start(). */
@@ -639,9 +643,10 @@
 	}
 
 	/*
-	 * The root PD has been restarted since this user PD was spawned and
-	 * nothing spawned it again (respawn_userpds=0): the running firmware
-	 * never hosted it and will never answer a stop request.
+	 * The root PD has been restarted since this user PD was spawned, yet
+	 * it was not torn down with it. The root's crash stop tears every up
+	 * user PD down, so this is only a guard: the running firmware never
+	 * hosted this PD and will never answer a stop request.
 	 */
 	stale = upd->spawned_gen != wcss->boot_gen;
 	if (stale)
